sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/genotype-correction-ray-interray-null-withdrawn-and-narrowed> a delphi:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Withdrawn and narrowed: ray-to-interray signalling IS established in the zebrafish caudal fin by grafting and niche experiments, so this thread's claim that no such arrow could be sourced in any fin of any fish was false; what survives is the much narrower statement that no source names a ligand in one compartment and its receptor in the other" ;
    dct:description "Status: correction: the earlier universal negative is withdrawn as refuted by findable literature; the narrowed claim that replaces it is a measured null against a stated criterion. WHAT WE SAID. A draft record from this thread asserted: \"No signalling arrow between a fin ray and the inter-ray tissue could be sourced in any fin of any fish.\" WHAT THE LITERATURE SAYS. That is false, and one query found three papers. Murciano 2002 (PMID 12482711) used single ray ablations and heterotopic graftings and concluded that \"the interray blastema is necessary for a proper bifurcation of each ray\", also showing that interray tissue and surrounding epidermis \"can modulate the length of the ray\", with msxA and msxD in ray and interray epidermis under control of the ray blastema and bmp4 named as a candidate signal. Blum 2015 (PMID 26253402) showed a Cyp26a1 retinoic-acid-degrading niche \"orchestrates ray-interray organization by spatially restricting osteoblasts\", with preosteoblasts invading neighbouring interrays when it is disrupted. Cardeira 2018 (PMID 29441573) associates kcnk5b, fibroblast growth factor receptor 1 and the sonic hedgehog pathway with inter-ray control under ray-dependent regulation. Ray-interray signalling is therefore established, by experiment, in the caudal fin. WHAT SURVIVES. The criterion the search actually applied was narrower than the sentence it produced: a source naming a LIGAND in one compartment and ITS RECEPTOR in the other. On that criterion the null holds against these three papers: bmp4 is offered explicitly as a \"candidate signal\" with no receptor named in the opposite compartment, Cyp26a1 is an enzyme establishing a diffusible-signal niche rather than a ligand-receptor pair across the boundary, and Cardeira names a receptor (Fgfr1) and a pathway but no ligand localised to the opposing compartment. The narrowed claim is therefore: no fetched source resolves ray-interray signalling to a named ligand-receptor pair across the boundary, and none of this work is in the pectoral fin. WHAT CHANGED. The universal sentence is withdrawn. The three papers are cited here as adjacent work the narrowed claim does not cover, and the project must say \"not resolved to a ligand-receptor pair\" rather than \"no arrow exists\". THE CAUSE, which is a process finding and not just a fact finding. The thread's own gap text recorded that one of these three papers was read \"title only\", and the thread had already cited that paper elsewhere. A title-only read was allowed to support a universal negative. A universal negative needs a stated criterion and a full read of anything it would contradict; the criterion existed in the gap text but was not carried into the headline, where a weaker, broader sentence took its place. Found by this project's own nanopub curation pass, not by the thread that wrote it. What it does not establish: The narrowed claim does not establish that no ligand-receptor pair operates across the ray-interray boundary: three abstracts are not a literature search, and the original search was bounded by what the fetcher could reach. It does not establish anything about the pectoral fin, where none of this work was done. And it does not rescue the withdrawn sentence: ray-interray signalling is real, demonstrated by grafting, and the project was wrong to say otherwise. Pre-registration: none for the correction. The withdrawn claim had a criterion stated in the thread's own gap text, which is what made the overreach detectable." ;
    delphi:context "The original record came from the interactions run, which produced 30 supported arrows with 12 graded mechanism, none of them ray-to-interray. That part stands: no arrow of this kind is drawn in genotype/interactions.json, and the three papers above would not have supplied one under its ligand-receptor criterion. All three are caudal-fin work, so the separate statement that every regeneration arrow in this project comes from the caudal fin and not the pectoral is unaffected." ;
    delphi:hasEvidence <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/genotype-correction-ray-interray-null-withdrawn-and-narrowed> ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-genotype-thread-claude-fable-5-1> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/genotype-correction-ray-interray-null-withdrawn-and-narrowed> a delphi:EvidenceItem ;
    rdfs:label "The three papers that refute the universal claim, tested against the record's own narrower criterion" ;
    dct:description "Abstracts fetched 2026-10-31 (genotype/cache/calls_rayinterray.json) and read against the criterion recorded in genotype/interactions.json gaps." ;
    delphi:statisticalTest "Re-check: the three PMIDs named by the curation pass were fetched through the thread's logged fetcher and their abstracts read, then tested against the record's own criterion (a named ligand in one compartment and its receptor in the other). No statistical test." ;
    dct:source <https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12482711/>, <https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26253402/>, <https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29441573/> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom sub:interactions ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://delphibio.org/t/genotype/data.json> .
  <https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12482711/> a prov:Entity ;
    dct:title "Ray-interray interactions during fin regeneration of Danio rerio" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Murciano C 2002 Dev Biol" ;
    dct:identifier "PMID 12482711" , "doi:10.1016/s0012-1606(02)00075-0" ;
    delphi:context "abstract only: the full text could not be fetched" ;
    rdfs:comment "We present evidence suggesting that the interray blastema is necessary for a proper bifurcation of each ray during regeneration in Danio rerio" .
  <https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26253402/> a prov:Entity ;
    dct:title "Retinoic acid signaling spatially restricts osteoblasts and controls ray-interray organization during zebrafish fin regeneration" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Blum N 2015 Development" ;
    dct:identifier "PMID 26253402" , "doi:10.1242/dev.120212" ;
    delphi:context "abstract only: the full text could not be fetched" ;
    rdfs:comment "we demonstrate that a retinoic acid (RA)-degrading niche is established by Cyp26a1 in the proximal basal epidermal layer that orchestrates ray-interray organization by spatially restricting osteoblasts" .
  <https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29441573/> a prov:Entity ;
    dct:title "Widening control of fin inter-rays in zebrafish and inferences about actinopterygian fins" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Cardeira J 2018 R Soc Open Sci" ;
    dct:identifier "PMID 29441573" , "doi:10.1098/rsos.171553" ;
    delphi:context "abstract only: the full text could not be fetched" ;
    rdfs:comment "The present work associates the activity of the cell membrane potassium channel kcnk5b, the fibroblast growth factor receptor 1 and the sonic hedgehog pathway to the control of several cell functions involved in inter-ray wound healing or dorsoventral regeneration of the zebrafish caudal fin" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/genotype-correction-ray-interray-null-withdrawn-and-narrowed> schema:additionalProperty sub:m0 .
  sub:m0 a schema:PropertyValue ; schema:propertyID "status" ; schema:value "correction: the earlier universal negative is withdrawn as refuted by findable literature; the narrowed claim that replaces it is a measured null against a stated criterion" ; schema:description "status of this record in the words of AGENTS.md (supported, refuted, mixed, inconclusive, or a descriptive status)" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/genotype-correction-ray-interray-null-withdrawn-and-narrowed> schema:additionalProperty sub:m1 .
